  Slow performance adding to a large Dask-graph
I was replacing a delayed object (`A` in the example), with a Dask array of 1000 chunks (`B` in the example). This increased the bottom of the Dask-graph with a factor of 1000.
The result was that the time for constructing the graph became unpleasantly long.
Testing some more, the speed of adding elements to the Dask-graph seems to be heavily dependent on the prior size of the graph (See the result below). (Line-)Profiling the code, didn't gave me one particular function to blame.
I would have expected it to be more depended on the amount of nodes added, and less on the prior size of the Dask-graph.

Is there something I can change to speed this up?
Is it possible to construct parts of a Dask-graph separately, and connecting them afterwards? Thereby applying the checks and optimizations on the Dask-graph only once, and not 1000 times.

Note: Computing the graph with a large N_prior in this example, leads to a recursion error.
```python
